        "committer": {
          "name": "Andreas Dilger",
          "email": "adilger@thelustrecollective.com",
          "date": "2026-11-25 08:56:10.000000000",
          "tz": -360
        },
        "subject": "LU-21229 ptlrpc: start threads when threads_min is raised",
        "message": "LU-21229 ptlrpc: start threads when threads_min is raised\n\nWriting SERVICE.threads_min to a running service only changed the\nlower limit for threads_max and started no threads, since a running\nservice adds threads only when one of its threads wakes up and finds\nthe others busy.  On a server that had just started with few threads\nin a partition, raising threads_min had no effect, and requests that\ntake long to handle could queue behind each other.\n\nStart the missing threads in each CPU partition at once, up to\nthreads_max (ptlrpc_start_threads_min()), with the loop that starts\na service's threads when it is set up (ptlrpc_svcpt_start_threads()).\nptlrpc_start_thread() checks srv_is_stopping again under scp_lock, so\na thread started from outside the service does not join a partition\nwhose threads were already stopped, and a partition whose threads\nwere not started yet is left to ptlrpc_start_threads().\nptlrpc_unregister_service() removes the service's sysfs files before\nit stops the threads, so that it waits for a threads_min write that\nis still starting a thread, which would otherwise wake up on a\nthread that was freed.\n\nAdd man pages for threads_min, threads_max and threads_started,\nhosted under the ldlm_cbd service, which also runs on clients, with\nlinks for every other service, and check in conf-sanity test_53a and\ntest_53b that a raised threads_min starts its threads.\n\nTest-Parameters: trivial testlist=conf-sanity env=ONLY=\"53a 53b\"\nSigned-off-by: Andreas Dilger <adilger@thelustrecollective.com>\nAssisted-by: ClaudeCode:claude-opus-5-5 ltvm llm_code_and_review_tools\nChange-Id: I452ec426a7424fb188e8a4dbda29806c713ebbe5\n"
      },
      "branch": "refs/heads/master"
    }
